---
import RevealLayout from "../../layouts/PresentationLayout.astro";
import {
  Probador,
  GarmentIcon,
  ParenthesesCounter,
  DEFAULT_GARMENT_COLORS,
} from "../../components/cave/you-cannot-take-off-a-jacket-you-never-wore";
import RevealCode from "../../components/RevealCode";
import CheeseTick from "../../components/icons/CheeseTick.astro";
import CheeseCross from "../../components/icons/CheeseCross.astro";
---

<RevealLayout
  title="You Can't Take Off a Jacket You Never Wore"
  description="Solving balanced parentheses with a dressing room metaphor"
  theme="dark"
>
  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      It might sound odd at first, but there is a close relationship between
      trying on clothes and knowing if a sequence of parentheses is valid.
    </aside>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="flex flex-col items-center justify-center h-full gap-8">
      <aside class="notes">
        They look similar. They use the same symbols. But one of them breaks.
      </aside>
      <div class="grid grid-rows-2 gap-40 text-center">
        <code class="text-6xl tracking-wider"> (()()) </code>
        <code class="text-6xl tracking-wider"> ())() </code>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <aside class="notes">Intro here</aside>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="flex flex-col items-center justify-center h-full gap-8">
      <aside class="notes">
        We are given a line of parentheses. The question is simple: is this line
        well formed? Every opening parenthesis must be closed. And nothing
        should close before it's opened.
      </aside>
      <div class="grid grid-rows-2 gap-40 text-center">
        <div class="grid grid-cols-2 items-center gap-4">
          <code class="text-6xl tracking-wider"> (()()) </code>
          <div class="fragment fade-in text-6xl" data-fragment-index="1">
            <CheeseTick class="w-24 mx-auto" />
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="grid grid-cols-2 items-center gap-4">
          <code class="text-6xl tracking-wider"> ())() </code>
          <div class="fragment fade-in text-6xl" data-fragment-index="1">
            <CheeseCross class="w-24 mx-auto" />
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      The first thing we tried was counting. Parentheses come in pairs. So we
      count how many open, and how many close.
      <!-- () -->
      If the numbers match... maybe we are done. And in many cases, we are.
      <!-- (()()) -->
      When the numbers do not match, the sequence is clearly invalid. If the number
      of opening parentheses is greater than the number of closing parentheses, something
      is missing.
      <!-- (()() -->
      If the number of closing parentheses is greater than the number of opening parentheses,
      something is extra.
      <!-- (()())) -->
      But then there is this.
      <!-- )( -->
      Same count. Nothing missing. Nothing extra. And still, completely broken. So
      counting helps. But it doesn't tell the whole story. Order matters.
    </aside>
    <div class="flex flex-col items-center justify-center h-full">
      <ParenthesesCounter
        client:load
        mode="count"
        title="Count Opening & Closing"
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      So instead of counting everything at the end, we follow the line as it
      unfolds. We keep track of how many opening parentheses we have seen that
      are still pending to be closed. If we try to close more parentheses than
      we have opened at any point, we know the sequence is invalid.
      <!-- ()) -->
      If we finish the line and there are still opening parentheses pending to be
      closed, the sequence is also invalid.
      <!-- (() -->
      This works well for a single type of parenthesis. But what if we have more than
      one type? We have parentheses, brackets, and curly braces. It might seem that
      it works the same way.
      <!-- ([{}]) -->
      But look at this one.
      <!-- (] -->
      So order matters, and type matters.
    </aside>
    <div class="flex flex-col items-center justify-center h-full">
      <ParenthesesCounter
        client:load
        mode="balance"
        title="Tracking Pending to Close"
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      We can try to keep track of how many opening parentheses of each type we
      have seen that are still pending to be closed.
      <!-- (] -->
      This example is no longer a problem. However, it's easy to find a new issue.
      <!-- ([)] -->
      We are trying to close a parenthesis when there is still an unclosed bracket.
      So we need to remember the most recent unclosed opener.
    </aside>
    <div class="flex flex-col items-center justify-center h-full">
      <ParenthesesCounter
        client:load
        mode="multi-count"
        title="Counter Per Type"
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      Let's use an analogy to make this idea clearer. Think of a changing room.
      You walk in, and you put one t-shirt on. Then a sweater. Then a jacket.
      After looking at yourself in the mirror, you start taking your clothes
      off. You have to take off the jacket first. Then the sweater. Then the
      t-shirt.
    </aside>
    <div class="flex flex-col items-center justify-center h-full">
      <Probador
        client:load
        actions={[
          { type: "PUT", garment: "T" },
          { type: "PUT", garment: "S" },
          { type: "PUT", garment: "J" },
          { type: "TAKE_OFF", garment: "J" },
          { type: "TAKE_OFF", garment: "S" },
          { type: "TAKE_OFF", garment: "T" },
        ]}
        displayMode="none"
        showTypeCounters={false}
        autoPlay={false}
        showControls={true}
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      Let's try a different sequence. T-Shirt, Sweater, Jacket, and... it's not
      possible to take off the sweater before the jacket. The last thing you put
      on is the first thing you take off.
    </aside>
    <div class="flex flex-col items-center justify-center h-full">
      <Probador
        client:load
        actions={[
          { type: "PUT", garment: "T" },
          { type: "PUT", garment: "S" },
          { type: "PUT", garment: "J" },
          { type: "TAKE_OFF", garment: "S" },
        ]}
        displayMode="none"
        showTypeCounters={false}
        autoPlay={false}
        showControls={true}
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      This is exactly how our problem works. Instead of stacking clothes one on
      top of another, we are stacking opening parentheses. When we see a closing
      parenthesis, we need to make sure it matches the most recent opening
      parenthesis we have seen. We repeat this process until we reach the end,
      making sure we finish in the same state we started.
    </aside>
    <div class="flex flex-col items-center justify-center h-full">
      <Probador
        client:load
        actions={[
          { type: "PUT", garment: "T" },
          { type: "PUT", garment: "S" },
          { type: "PUT", garment: "J" },
          { type: "TAKE_OFF", garment: "J" },
          { type: "TAKE_OFF", garment: "S" },
          { type: "PUT", garment: "S" },
          { type: "TAKE_OFF", garment: "S" },
          { type: "TAKE_OFF", garment: "T" },
        ]}
        displayMode="none"
        showParentheses={true}
        autoPlay={false}
        showControls={true}
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      This pile of symbols is what we call a stack. The last item added to the
      stack is the first one to be removed. Last In, First Out.
    </aside>
    <div class="flex flex-col items-center justify-center h-full">
      <Probador
        client:load
        actions={[
          { type: "PUT", garment: "T" },
          { type: "PUT", garment: "S" },
          { type: "TAKE_OFF", garment: "S" },
          { type: "PUT", garment: "J" },
          { type: "TAKE_OFF", garment: "J" },
          { type: "TAKE_OFF", garment: "T" },
        ]}
        displayMode="stack"
        showParentheses={true}
        autoPlay={false}
        showControls={true}
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="code" class="text-lg">
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        Let's see how this works in Python code. We start with an empty stack.
        This is where we keep the parentheses that are still open. In Python, we
        implement the stack as a list.
      </p>
      <p>
        We also create a mapping from closing parentheses to their corresponding
        opening ones. This mapping defines which opening parenthesis we expect
        to find on the stack when we encounter a closing parenthesis.
      </p>
      <p>We then go through the input line, one character at a time.</p>
      <p>
        If the character is an opening parenthesis, we push it onto the stack.
        We are simply remembering it. If the character is not an opening
        parenthesis, we assume it is a closing one. We assume no letters,
        numbers or other symbols will appear in the input.
      </p>
      <p>
        At that point, we ask: do we have something to close? If the stack is
        empty, we stop immediately.
      </p>
      <p>
        If there is something to close, we take the most recent opening
        parenthesis. We use pop to get and remove the last item from the stack.
      </p>
      <p>
        We then use the mapping to check if this opening parenthesis matches the
        closing one we just encountered. If it does not match, the sequence is
        invalid.
      </p>
      <p>
        We repeat this until we reach the end of the line. At the very end, only
        one thing matters: did we close everything we opened?
      </p>
      <p>
        The time complexity is linear because we process each character in the
        input string exactly once. The space complexity is also linear in the
        worst case, as we may need to store all opening parentheses in the stack
        if they are never closed. So we have an efficient solution.
      </p>
    </aside>
    <RevealCode
      client:only="react"
      animation="fade"
      code={`def is_valid_parentheses(text: str) -> bool:
    # STEP: We create empty stack to remember opening parentheses
    stack = []
    # STEP: Mapping of closing to opening parentheses
    matching = {
        ')': '(',
        ']': '[',
        '}': '{',
    }

    # STEP: Process each character in the input text
    for char in text:
        # STEP: If we see an opening parenthesis, we remember it
        if char in '([{':
            stack.append(char)
        # STEP: If we see a closing parenthesis
        else:  # we assume char in ')]}'
            # STEP: There must be something to close
            if not stack:
                return False

            # STEP: It must close the last opened parenthesis
            last_open = stack.pop()

            # STEP: And it must be the correct type
            if matching[char] != last_open:
                return False

    # STEP: At the end, nothing should be left open
    return len(stack) == 0`}
      `
    />
  </section>

  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      The key takeaway is simple. With one type of parenthesis, counting works.
      But with multiple types, we need to remember the order in which they were
      opened. A stack is the perfect data structure for this job.
    </aside>
    <div class="flex flex-col items-center justify-center h-full gap-8">
      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold">Counter vs Stack</h2>
      <div class="grid grid-cols-2 gap-50 text-xl mt-4">
        <div class="text-center fragment">
          <p class="text-amber-400 font-bold text-2xl mb-4">Single type</p>
          <p class="text-gray-300">Just count!</p>
          <code class="text-green-400 text-3xl block mt-4">( ( ) ( ) )</code>
        </div>
        <div class="text-center fragment">
          <p class="text-amber-400 font-bold text-2xl mb-4">Multiple types</p>
          <p class="text-gray-300">Need a stack!</p>
          <code class="text-3xl block mt-4">
            <span class="text-green-400">(</span>
            <span class="text-blue-400">[</span>
            <span class="text-purple-400">{`{`}</span>
            <span class="text-purple-400">{`}`}</span>
            <span class="text-blue-400">]</span>
            <span class="text-green-400">)</span>
          </code>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="follow-up-code" class="text-lg">
    <aside class="notes">
      There is another way to solve this. Same idea. Different perspective. I'll
      leave it here... Leave a comment if you can figure out why it works.
    </aside>
    <RevealCode
      client:only="react"
      animation="fade"
      code={`def is_valid_parentheses(text: str) -> bool:
    stack = []

    for char in text:
        if char in "([{":
            stack.append(char)
        else:
            if not stack:
                return False

            last_open = stack.pop()

            diff = ord(char) - ord(last_open)
            if diff != 1 and diff != 2:
                return False

    return len(stack) == 0
`}
    />
  </section>

  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      This was today's Cheese Byte. Remember, the code matters, but the idea
      comes first.
    </aside>
    <iframe
      src="https://cheesebytes.com/cave/parentheses-balancing/"
      width="1080"
      height="720"></iframe>
  </section>
</RevealLayout>
